(Bug #71's other half of the ledger: the rows that were already right and
 had to stay right when the specifier dispatch was rebuilt to consult the
 value's type first.

 The radix rows are what a base specifier is FOR - a whole number written
 in another base - and they are the working neighbour that proves the fix
 removed a wrong answer rather than the feature. A boolean renders as its
 1/0 (LANGUAGE.md:2229), so it is a whole number here too.

 The `value`, list and map rows are the runtime-tagged path, which honours
 no specifier at all and renders the value: that behaviour is deliberate
 (#36 records it) and is not something a type check can second-guess, since
 a `value`'s type is not known until it runs.

 The sink rows are LANGUAGE.md:3226 - the same hole, printed and built into
 a buffer, has to read the same both times.)
a number called n is 255.
a number called eight is 8.
a boolean called ready is true.

Print "{n:x}".
Print "{n:X}".
Print "{n:b}".
Print "{eight:o}".
Print "{n:04x}".
Print "{n:8x}".
Print "{ready:x}".
Print "{ready:06}".
Print "{n:06}".
Print "{n:6}".

a value called held is 2.5.
a value called worded is "words".
Print "{held:06}".
Print "{worded:x}".

a list called items is [1, 2].
a map called ages is {"bo": 3}.
Print "{items:x}".
Print "{ages:.2}".

a buffer called out is 64 bytes in size.
copy "{n:x} {n:06} {n:.2}" to out.
Print "{out}".
